  "intro": "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links derivatives and integrals. Part 1: if $F(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,dt$, then $F'(x)=f(x)$, so differentiating an accumulation undoes the integral. Part 2: if $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$, then $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=F(b)-F(a)$. Part 2 turns area problems into antiderivative evaluations. With a variable upper limit $g(x)$, the chain rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_a^{g(x)} f(t)\\,dt=f(g(x))\\,g'(x)$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_0^3 x^2\\,dx$ using the Fundamental Theorem.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "An antiderivative of $x^2$ is $F(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}$.",
          "Apply Part 2: $F(3)-F(0)=\\frac{27}{3}-0$.",
          "So the integral equals $9$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\int_0^3 x^2\\,dx=9$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "If $F(x)=\\int_0^x \\sin(t^2)\\,dt$, find $F'(x)$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "By Part 1, differentiating the accumulation returns the integrand evaluated at the upper limit.",
          "So $F'(x)=\\sin(x^2)$."
        ],
        "answer": "$F'(x)=\\sin(x^2)$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Find $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\int_1^{x^2} \\sqrt{t}\\,dt$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The upper limit is $g(x)=x^2$, so use the chain-rule form of Part 1.",
          "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_1^{g(x)} \\sqrt{t}\\,dt=\\sqrt{g(x)}\\cdot g'(x)=\\sqrt{x^2}\\cdot 2x$.",
          "For $x>0$, $\\sqrt{x^2}=x$, giving $x\\cdot 2x=2x^2$."
        ],
        "answer": "$2x^2$ (for $x>0$)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_1^4 2x\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Antiderivative $x^2$; $4^2-1^2=16-1=15$."
        ],
        "answer": "$15$"
